{
  "requirement": "The repository is a Cargo workspace with exactly two first-party packages:\n`habeas` at the repository root and `trust-but-verify` in\n`crates/trust-but-verify/`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The requirement text gives one structural obligation: the repository is a Cargo workspace with exactly two first-party packages, `habeas` at the root and `trust-but-verify` in `crates/trust-but-verify/`. An alternative that drops the `[workspace]` table, or lists extra members, would violate it, so the obligation is real. The root `Cargo.toml#table[workspace]/pair[members]` supports it, and the other items I read are consistent with it.\n\n- `Cargo.toml#table[workspace]/pair[members]` is `members = [\"crates/trust-but-verify\"]`.\n- The root `Cargo.toml#table[package]/pair[name]` is `habeas`. A root manifest that has both `[workspace]` and `[package]` makes the root package an implicit member, so `habeas` is the first package.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/Cargo.toml#table[package]/pair[name]` is `trust-but-verify`, which matches the listed member.\n- `Cargo.toml#table[workspace]/pair[exclude]` is `[\"vendor/habeas-rig\"]`. This keeps the vendored rig out of the workspace, so no third package is added.\n\nThe members list is explicit rather than a glob, so no other package under `crates/` can join. The root covers the whole obligation, and no other root is needed.\n\nbehavioral: The only obligation is structural and observable: the repository is a Cargo workspace whose first-party packages are exactly `habeas` at the root and `trust-but-verify` in `crates/trust-but-verify/`.\n\n`tests/repository.rs#fn[workspace_contains_frontend_and_sdk_but_not_the_vendor_fork]` covers it:\n- It runs `cargo metadata --no-deps --locked --offline` and keeps only the packages listed in `workspace_members`. It sorts their names and asserts they equal exactly `[\"habeas\", \"trust-but-verify\"]`. This enforces \"exactly two\" and the two names. An extra member, such as a vendor fork, or a missing member would fail.\n- It runs in `CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR`, the root package's directory, so `habeas` is the root package.\n- It asserts that `crates/trust-but-verify/src/lib.rs` is a file. It also asserts that the root manifest's `trust-but-verify` dependency has path `crates/trust-but-verify`. Together these tie `trust-but-verify` to its required directory.\n\nThe test does not compare the `trust-but-verify` package's `manifest_path` to that directory. A workspace member with that name in another directory would be an odd, unlikely violation, and the path dependency check makes it implausible. I do not count it as a gap. The test carries a `{test}` reference to the subject, so there is no marking gap.",
  "references": "5c1c13b8cee809e36cd1067ef434f65dbda72765ee9ebc8fa8ba8a3bd4ad88d1",
