        "rationale": "The requirement text gives one structural obligation: the repository is a Cargo workspace with exactly two first-party packages, `habeas` at the root and `trust-but-verify` in `crates/trust-but-verify/`. An alternative that drops the `[workspace]` table, or lists extra members, would violate it, so the obligation is real. The root `Cargo.toml#table[workspace]/pair[members]` supports it, and the other items I read are consistent with it.\n\n- `Cargo.toml#table[workspace]/pair[members]` is `members = [\"crates/trust-but-verify\"]`.\n- The root `Cargo.toml#table[package]/pair[name]` is `habeas`. A root manifest that has both `[workspace]` and `[package]` makes the root package an implicit member, so `habeas` is the first package.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/Cargo.toml#table[package]/pair[name]` is `trust-but-verify`, which matches the listed member.\n- `Cargo.toml#table[workspace]/pair[exclude]` is `[\"vendor/habeas-rig\"]`. This keeps the vendored rig out of the workspace, so no third package is added.\n\nThe members list is explicit rather than a glob, so no other package under `crates/` can join. The root covers the whole obligation, and no other root is needed."
