        "rationale": "The only obligation is structural and observable: the repository is a Cargo workspace whose first-party packages are exactly `habeas` at the root and `trust-but-verify` in `crates/trust-but-verify/`.\n\n`tests/repository.rs#fn[workspace_contains_frontend_and_sdk_but_not_the_vendor_fork]` covers it:\n- It runs `cargo metadata --no-deps --locked --offline` and keeps only the packages listed in `workspace_members`. It sorts their names and asserts they equal exactly `[\"habeas\", \"trust-but-verify\"]`. This enforces \"exactly two\" and the two names. An extra member, such as a vendor fork, or a missing member would fail.\n- It runs in `CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR`, the root package's directory, so `habeas` is the root package.\n- It asserts that `crates/trust-but-verify/src/lib.rs` is a file. It also asserts that the root manifest's `trust-but-verify` dependency has path `crates/trust-but-verify`. Together these tie `trust-but-verify` to its required directory.\n\nThe test does not compare the `trust-but-verify` package's `manifest_path` to that directory. A workspace member with that name in another directory would be an odd, unlikely violation, and the path dependency check makes it implausible. I do not count it as a gap. The test carries a `{test}` reference to the subject, so there is no marking gap."
